    <slide>
      <title>The Jesus Creed</title>
      <p>Hear, O Israel: The Lord our God, the Lord is one.</p>
      <p>Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all</p>
      <p>your soul and with all your mind and with all your strength.</p>
      <p>The second is this: Love your neighbor as yourself.</p>
    </slide>
    <section>
      <title>Height and winning at tennis</title>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title>Step 1: Ask a research question</title>
      <slide>
        <p>Based on trends observed in other sports, researchers were interested in exploring how heights of 
  winning tennis players might be changing over time, given that height may be an advantage to winning at tennis.</p>
      </slide>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title>Step 2: Design a study and collect data</title>
      <slide>
        <p>To investigate this question, <url href="https://www.topendsports.com/sport/tennis/anthropometry-wimbledon.htm">
  researchers gathered data</url> on heights of the winning tennis players at Wimbledon, a prestigious tennis 
  tournament held each year from the 1880s through 2019</p>
      </slide>
      <slide>
        <title>Exercise 1</title>
        <!--Question 1-->
        <p>Identify (be specific) the observational units in this study.</p>
      </slide>
      <slide>
        <title>Exercise 2</title>
        <!--Question 2-->
        <p>What is the explanatory variable? Is it categorical or quantitative?</p>
      </slide>
      <slide>
        <title>Exercise 3</title>
        <!--Question 3-->
        <p>What is the response variable? Is it categorical or quantitative?</p>
      </slide>
      <slide>
        <title>Exercise 4</title>
        <!--Question 4-->
        <p>In your own words, explain what it would mean for there to be an association between
the explanatory and response variables in this study.</p>
      </slide>
      <slide>
        <title>Exercise 5</title>
        <!--Question 5-->
        <p>Is this study an experiment or an observational study? Why?</p>
      </slide>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title>Step 3: Explore the data</title>
      <slide>
